><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20251110102834+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20251110102842+10'00'" name="3b1ae693-6193-43b6-8bb8-360f0271d0c1" page="5" coords="313.001495,161.340485,575.937439,161.340485,313.001495,150.310562,575.937439,150.310562,313.001495,150.285370,575.956543,150.285370,313.001495,139.255447,575.956543,139.255447,313.001495,139.287262,576.001953,139.287262,313.001495,128.257339,576.001953,128.257339,313.001495,128.232147,576.000427,128.232147,313.001495,117.202225,576.000427,117.202225,313.001495,117.177032,575.936646,117.177032,313.001495,106.147110,575.936646,106.147110,313.001495,106.121918,576.005127,106.121918,313.001495,95.091995,576.005127,95.091995,313.001495,95.066803,576.001770,95.066803,313.001495,84.036880,576.001770,84.036880,313.001495,84.011688,575.951660,84.011688,313.001495,72.981766,575.951660,72.981766,313.001495,73.013580,575.948120,73.013580,313.001495,61.983658,575.948120,61.983658,313.001495,61.958466,575.961853,61.958466,313.001495,50.928543,575.961853,50.928543,313.001495,50.903366,575.947266,50.903366,313.001495,39.873444,575.947266,39.873444" rect="312.656799,39.528748,576.349854,161.685181" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Integrating heat sessions into the yearly training plan requires&#xD;careful consideration to: (1) maximize physiological adaptation,&#xD;(2) compliment periodization of training, and (3) acclimatize for&#xD;competition in specific environmental conditions. For example, in&#xD;the present study, STHA in humid conditions promoted greater&#xD;physiological adaptations demonstrating an adaptive response to&#xD;daily total work from days 1 to 5 but a restricted overall work&#xD;output (and intensity) for the 5 days compared with dry conditions,&#xD;thereby potentially limiting training quality. Therefore, periodizing&#xD;blocks of humid STHA prior to blocks of high-intensity work may&#xD;augment training adaptations. The expanded PV may possibly </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>increase cardiac filling and stroke volume, causing a greater stretch&#xD;on the myocardium and enhancing cardiovascular adaptations&#xD;during subsequent blocks of high intensity following STHA.&#xD;Alternatively, utilization of dry STHA may be programmed when&#xD;heat acclimation/exposure is integrated into a block of high training&#xD;load (as total work completed was higher) or alternatively, prior to&#xD;competition in a dry environment (due to preferential thermoregulatory&#xD;adaptations).</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="612.000000,69.340485,796.000000,161.340485"
/></highlight
